/* ---------------- Article 21 ---------------- */
const integrationSections:ArticleSection[]=[
{id:"integration-products",title:"Why fintech products are integration products",answer:"A customer may see one application, but the experience depends on how identity, payment, data, execution, compliance and reporting systems coordinate behind it.",paragraphs:["Behind a single fintech app there can be identity systems, payment providers, market and financial data vendors, exchanges or execution venues, document and verification services, internal databases and reporting services. Each has its own data model, availability and failure behaviour.","APIs are the mechanism for connecting them. They are not the strategy. The strategy decides which systems participate in each workflow, which system owns each state, what data moves, how failures are handled and where provider-specific logic lives. The general enterprise approach is covered in the system integration strategy pillar; this article focuses on the financial ecosystem."],links:[integrationPillar]},
{id:"integration-map",title:"The Fintech Integration Map",variant:"framework",answer:"For every connection around the platform, answer three questions: what moves, who owns the state, and what happens if it fails.",subsections:[{title:"Identity & verification",paragraphs:["Moves identity attributes and verification outcomes. The identity service owns authentication state."]},{title:"Payments",paragraphs:["Moves payment instructions and status. The provider owns processing state; the platform owns the customer-facing record."]},{title:"Market / financial data",paragraphs:["Moves prices, reference and fundamental data. The platform owns the normalized model it shows customers."]},{title:"Trading / execution",paragraphs:["Moves orders and acknowledgements. The execution system owns execution state; the platform owns positions it records."]},{title:"Compliance / documents",paragraphs:["Moves documents, signatures and checks. Ownership of each check must be explicit."]},{title:"Portfolio / reporting",paragraphs:["Moves derived records for statements and reports. Depends on reconciled upstream state."]},{title:"Internal data / operations",paragraphs:["Moves operational records to support, finance and analytics teams."]}]},
{id:"provider-boundary",title:"Keep external-provider complexity out of customer-facing applications",answer:"A middleware or internal API layer can absorb provider differences so web and mobile applications depend on stable internal contracts.",bullets:["Provider abstraction behind a consistent internal model","Normalized responses and error handling","Central validation before requests leave the platform","One place to monitor provider behaviour","Easier future provider substitution","Reduced frontend coupling to external APIs"],paragraphs:["Middleware is not mandatory for every architecture. A small product with one provider may not need it. It becomes more valuable as providers, channels and workflows multiply."]},
{id:"reliability-chain",title:"The Fintech Integration Reliability Chain",variant:"framework",answer:"Authenticate → Validate → Send → Acknowledge → Process → Persist → Confirm → Reconcile → Observe.",paragraphs:["This is a reasoning model, not a mandatory protocol. The exact stages vary by workflow. Its purpose is to make each step explicit so the team can ask what happens if that step fails: is the request retried, is the customer told, is the state marked as pending, and who is alerted?"]},
{id:"identity",title:"Identity integration",paragraphs:["Identity sits at the start of almost every financial workflow. Centralized authentication, well-managed sessions and token-based access allow web, mobile and partner channels to behave consistently. Verification outcomes from external identity services should be recorded with their source and time so later decisions can be explained. Implementation specifics belong to a security design, not a general article."]},
{id:"payments",title:"Payment integration",answer:"A frontend \"success\" screen is not the same as end-to-end financial consistency.",paragraphs:["A payment lifecycle typically runs: initiate, external processing, response, internal state update, transaction recording and reconciliation. Provider responses may be delayed, arrive by callback or change later. The platform should represent pending states honestly and treat confirmation and reconciliation as separate steps from the customer's click."],bullets:["Initiate","External processing","Response or callback","Internal state update","Transaction recording","Reconciliation"]},
{id:"market-data",title:"Market and financial-data integration",paragraphs:["Platforms often combine several data providers for prices, reference data and fundamentals. Providers differ in identifiers, formats, timing and coverage. Normalizing and validating data behind a provider abstraction gives customers one consistent model and lets the platform add or replace sources without rewriting screens."]},
{id:"trading",title:"Trading and execution integration",paragraphs:["At a high level, an order moves through request, validation, the execution system, acknowledgement, execution outcome, portfolio or position update and reporting. Each hand-off can succeed, fail or remain pending, and the platform must know which. This article describes system coordination only; it does not address trading strategy or offer financial advice."],bullets:["Order request","Validation","Execution system","Acknowledgement","Execution outcome","Portfolio / position update","Reporting"]},
{id:"documents",title:"Document and verification integrations",paragraphs:["Onboarding may involve KYC-related systems, eSign or document providers, bank-account verification and identity workflows. The integration concern is to know which provider performed which check, what the outcome was and where evidence is stored. Regulatory obligations remain the responsibility of the regulated business and its advisers."]},
{id:"sync-async",title:"Synchronous vs asynchronous integrations",answer:"Use synchronous calls when the customer needs an immediate answer and asynchronous patterns when work is long-running, retriable or driven by external status.",table:{headers:["Need","Likely pattern"],rows:[["Immediate validation","Synchronous API"],["Long-running or retriable work","Asynchronous queue or event"],["External status update","Callback, webhook or event"],["Reporting and analytics","Pipeline or batch"],["High-value state reconciliation","Explicit reconciliation workflow"]]},paragraphs:["This mapping is directional. Real workflows often combine patterns."]},
{id:"idempotency",title:"Idempotency and duplicate prevention",answer:"A retry should never accidentally create the same financial action twice.",paragraphs:["Networks time out and providers respond late, so retries are normal. Idempotency means the same request, sent more than once, has the same effect as sending it once. In practice this usually involves a stable request identifier that both sides recognize, and checks before creating a new record or instruction. Many payment providers publish their own idempotency mechanisms; the platform's internal services need the same discipline."]},
{id:"reconciliation",title:"Reconciliation",answer:"Internal and external records can disagree temporarily. Reconciliation verifies that they agree in the end—and flags when they do not.",paragraphs:["Reconciliation compares the expected state, the recorded state and the external confirmation, and identifies missing or duplicate conditions. It matters most in payments, trading, reporting and verification, where a silent mismatch can surface later as a customer complaint or a reporting error. Design it as a routine workflow with owners, not as an emergency script."]},
{id:"observability",title:"Integration observability",bullets:["Request and correlation IDs across services and providers","Explicit status for each step","Structured logs","Recorded external responses","Retry counts and outcomes","Actionable alerts","An audit trail for significant actions"],paragraphs:["When a customer asks where their payment or order is, the team should be able to answer from the platform, not by searching several vendor dashboards."]},
{id:"in-practice",title:"What this looks like in practice",variant:"proof",paragraphs:["Xyncwave's anonymized Digital Brokerage Platform case study documents middleware connecting digital applications with exchanges, payment providers, market and fundamental-data providers and other third parties, alongside identity integrations, internal databases and reporting. It is evidence of experience with the integration concerns described here, within the anonymized boundary of the published case."],links:[brokerage,integrationSolution,cloud]},
];
